  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nVery comfortable and pleasant conditions return today into\ntomorrow as high pressure builds over the Northeast. Then, a\nBermuda high becomes anchored off the East Coast for the end of\nthe week. Persistent southwesterly flow around its periphery \nwill advect a warm and very humid air mass from the Gulf and \nwestern Atlantic into the Northeast with PWATs reaching 2 ST DEV\nabove normal per the NAEFS and ranking above the 90th percentile\nof the CFSR climatology for early August. \n\nA weak shortwave from the Southeast U.S becomes sheared out as \nit tracks within the southwest flow aloft into the Northeast \nwith broad troughing becoming established over the Canadian \nPrairie and the Great Lakes. Southwest winds aloft strengthen as\nthe Northeast is caught between the upstream troughing and \nBermuda high off shore supporting increased deep layer shear. \nThe moist air mass in the presence of at least some sunshine \nwill easily contribute to moderate instability. Mid and upper \nlevel forcing is weak but there are signs of a sfc pressure \ntrough tracking eastward off Lake Ontario which would provide a \nfocus for sfc-based convection on Thursday. Therefore, expecting\nisolated to scattered showers and storms to develop during the \ntraditional peak heating hours capable of producing heavy \ndownpours. Shower/storm coverage will increase should the sfc \ntrough push eastward. Favorable instability and sufficient deep \nlayer shear increases the chance for strong/severe storms with \nthe latest AI NWP Convective Hazard Forecast showing 15 - 30% \nchance for severe storms. Similar set- up for Friday but \nshower/storm coverage looks to increase as the upstream troughing\nand sfc boundary pushes eastward. These factors can also support\na greater risk for severe weather with the AI NWP Convective \nHazard Forecast showing 15-30% chance and even a narrow swath of\n30-45%. Otherwise, WPC maintains a widespread marginal risk for\nexcessive rainfall both days given potential for heavy \ndownpours where soils are saturated from recent heavy rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe combination of uncomfortable humidity levels and temperatures\nwarming slightly above normal Thursday and Friday increases the\nrisk for heat-related illness, especially in valley areas. \nLatest HeatRisk graphics show much of eastern NY and western \nNew England reaching the minor category on Thursday with minor\nto moderate on Friday. Latest NBM probabilistic guidance shows a\n40 to 60% chance for temperatures exceeding 85 degrees both days\nin valley areas with a 60 to 90% chance in the mid-Hudson\nValley. There is even a 15 to 30% chance that the mid-Hudson \nValley reaches or exceeds 90 degrees. There is a low to medium \nchance that these conditions will necessitate heat advisories \nwith the highest chance in the mid-Hudson Valley. Plan to avoid \nstrenuous outdoor activities both days, have a way to stay cool \nor access air conditioning and drink plenty of water, even if \nyou are not thirsty.",
